WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:01.534 align:start size:94% position:6%
-So, I feel as though
the last time you were on,

00:00:01.534 --> 00:00:03.370 align:start size:94% position:6%
we were talking
about the fact that

00:00:03.370 --> 00:00:04.070 size:81% position:41% line:92%
we were pretty quickly

00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:06.773 size:88% position:44%
going to have a nominee
for the Democratic Party

00:00:06.773 --> 00:00:08.174 line:92%
and all the news would shift

00:00:08.174 --> 00:00:10.877 align:start size:94% position:6%
to the horse race
of the presidential election

00:00:10.877 --> 00:00:12.112 line:92%
That has not been the case

00:00:12.112 --> 00:00:13.079 size:78% position:39% line:92%
Is it surreal to you,

00:00:13.079 --> 00:00:15.348 align:start size:94% position:6%
having covered this
from the very beginning,

00:00:15.348 --> 00:00:19.085 align:start size:94% position:6%
how certainly the Democratic
primary resolved itself?

00:00:19.085 --> 00:00:20.687 align:start size:94% position:6%
-It's so surreal
to think about,

00:00:20.687 --> 00:00:23.957 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know, 15 or 16 months
of intense focus.

00:00:23.957 --> 00:00:27.527
I mean, Kamala Harris'
big announcement in the Bay Area

00:00:27.527 --> 00:00:30.196 align:start size:94% position:6%
with thousands of people
and the first debate

00:00:30.196 --> 00:00:32.665 align:start size:94% position:6%
and the second debate
and, you know,

00:00:32.665 --> 00:00:34.401 align:start size:94% position:6%
John Delaney getting
his butt kicked

00:00:34.401 --> 00:00:36.202 align:start size:94% position:6%
[Chuckling]
by Elizabeth Warren --

00:00:36.202 --> 00:00:38.905 align:start size:94% position:6%
that all of that converged
to a video

00:00:38.905 --> 00:00:43.243
probably shot on an iPad in th
Vermont house of Bernie Sanders,

00:00:43.243 --> 00:00:47.380 align:start size:94% position:6%
beamed to the world amidst
the worst pandemic in 100 year

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:48.782 align:start size:94% position:6%
and the economy
on total lockdown

00:00:48.782 --> 00:00:52.085 align:start size:94% position:6%
and half of the world's
population essentially shelt--

00:00:52.085 --> 00:00:52.886 size:75% position:38% line:92%
Like, who -- It's --

00:00:52.886 --> 00:00:56.056
It was incomprehensible that
this is where it would end up,

00:00:56.056 --> 00:00:56.823 line:92%
but this is where it's ended up.

00:00:56.823 --> 00:01:01.294
And so, there's naturally
this way in which I think the --

00:01:01.294 --> 00:01:05.799 align:start size:94% position:6%
the urgency of the virus
is blotting out campaign news.

00:01:05.799 --> 00:01:08.468
But, also, it's completely
recast the dynamics of the race.

00:01:08.468 --> 00:01:12.172 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, the assumption
for those 15 months

00:01:12.172 --> 00:01:13.373 line:92%
and through impeachment was --

00:01:13.373 --> 00:01:17.677 size:81% position:41%
the fundamentals are,
this man is unpopular.

00:01:17.677 --> 00:01:19.646 line:92%
He's never been above 50%.

00:01:19.646 --> 00:01:21.714 align:start size:94% position:6%
He won with 46% of the vot
last time,

00:01:21.714 --> 00:01:26.152
but the economy is doing,
in a macro sense, pretty well --

00:01:26.152 --> 00:01:28.655 align:start size:94% position:6%
what does that mean
for the election?

00:01:28.655 --> 00:01:32.192
That is not the election
that we're having anymore.

00:01:32.192 --> 00:01:33.159 line:92%
So part of it is I think peopl

00:01:33.159 --> 00:01:35.128 align:start size:94% position:6%
haven't even gotten
their hands around,

00:01:35.128 --> 00:01:37.864 align:start size:94% position:6%
I certainly haven't
gotten my head around,

00:01:37.864 --> 00:01:39.265 size:81% position:41% line:92%
what is this election?

00:01:39.265 --> 00:01:40.166 line:92%
What's the substance of it

00:01:40.166 --> 00:01:42.268 align:start size:94% position:6%
Because the future
is so uncertain.

00:01:42.268 --> 00:01:43.503 align:start size:94% position:6%
I don't know what June
looks like.

00:01:43.503 --> 00:01:45.105 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I don't --
I sure as hell don't know

00:01:45.105 --> 00:01:46.106 size:88% position:44% line:92%
what October looks like.

00:01:46.106 --> 00:01:49.442
-It did seem as though the party
coalesced around the idea

00:01:49.442 --> 00:01:50.243 line:92%
of Biden as the candidate

00:01:50.243 --> 00:01:54.247 align:start size:94% position:6%
as sort of the safest contrast
to Donald Trump,

00:01:54.247 --> 00:01:57.283 align:start size:94% position:6%
like maybe not
the most idea-driven candidate

00:01:57.283 --> 00:01:57.784 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Yes.

00:01:57.784 --> 00:02:00.320 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Hopefully I'm not being unfai
in saying that.

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:01.187 align:start size:94% position:6%
-No, I think --
-And yet,

00:02:01.187 --> 00:02:05.191
now we're in a situation
that it is only contrast.

00:02:05.191 --> 00:02:08.394
It seems as though Joe Biden
who has not necessarily been out

00:02:08.394 --> 00:02:12.031
as much as you would expec
a nominee for the presidency

00:02:12.031 --> 00:02:13.533 size:81% position:41% line:92%
to be out in public --

00:02:13.533 --> 00:02:15.301 align:start size:94% position:6%
it seems like he's maybe
just stepping back

00:02:15.301 --> 00:02:16.836 align:start size:94% position:6%
and letting people
see the contrast.

00:02:16.836 --> 00:02:19.606
-I think that's part of it
I mean, I do think that --

00:02:19.606 --> 00:02:24.077
look, one way to think about i
is all re-election campaigns

00:02:24.077 --> 00:02:28.014 align:start size:94% position:6%
are referendums
on the incumbent, unavoidably.

00:02:28.014 --> 00:02:32.452 align:start size:94% position:6%
That dynamic seems like
it's going to be supersize

00:02:32.452 --> 00:02:33.086 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
in this case.

00:02:33.086 --> 00:02:35.788 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, it's going to be
a referendum on the incumben

00:02:35.788 --> 00:02:41.094 size:88% position:44%
and his handling of this
once-a-century calamity.

00:02:41.094 --> 00:02:44.564 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Chuckles ]
And so, at a certain level

00:02:44.564 --> 00:02:45.698 size:78% position:39% line:92%
it's like -- it is a,

00:02:45.698 --> 00:02:48.835 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Do you want to keep going
with this guy or not?"

00:02:48.835 --> 00:02:49.869 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
kind of election.

00:02:49.869 --> 00:02:55.241 align:start size:94% position:6%
And you have to be present
in illuminating your own ideas

00:02:55.241 --> 00:02:59.345
I think, as the opposition party
and as the opposition candidate.

00:02:59.345 --> 00:03:01.014 align:start size:94% position:6%
But it's also the case
that, like,

00:03:01.014 --> 00:03:03.116 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think that dynamic
fundamentally

00:03:03.116 --> 00:03:06.085 align:start size:94% position:6%
is a pretty inescapable on
at this point.

00:03:06.085 --> 00:03:09.222 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Can you talk real quick about
what happened in Wisconsin

00:03:09.222 --> 00:03:12.225 align:start size:94% position:6%
Because again, I think it go
dwarfed, obviously,

00:03:12.225 --> 00:03:13.626 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
by pandemic news.

00:03:13.626 --> 00:03:15.495 line:92%
But it was a really, truly -

00:03:15.495 --> 00:03:18.264
first,
crazy that the primary was held.

00:03:18.264 --> 00:03:21.568 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then sort of the outcome
seemed to be the reverse

00:03:21.568 --> 00:03:22.969 align:start size:94% position:6%
to what a lot of people
were expecting

00:03:22.969 --> 00:03:25.605 align:start size:94% position:6%
based on the fact
that the election didn't help.

00:03:25.605 --> 00:03:26.706 line:92%
-It was -- It was such a -

00:03:26.706 --> 00:03:30.677 align:start size:94% position:6%
I found it to be
a really upsetting undertaking

00:03:30.677 --> 00:03:32.812 line:92%
like, shockingly cynical.

00:03:32.812 --> 00:03:34.981 align:start size:94% position:6%
The basic dynamics were --
and I --

00:03:34.981 --> 00:03:35.882 line:92%
you know, back on March 17th

00:03:35.882 --> 00:03:39.852 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think were the last big
Democratic primary elections

00:03:39.852 --> 00:03:42.222 align:start size:94% position:6%
And that -- things were --
it was a little unclear

00:03:42.222 --> 00:03:43.256 align:start size:94% position:6%
they should have
gone forward then.

00:03:43.256 --> 00:03:45.625 align:start size:94% position:6%
We since heard the poll worker
in Illinois died.

00:03:45.625 --> 00:03:47.760
By the end --
but right up to that, I thought,

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:49.796 line:92%
"This is not a good idea."

00:03:49.796 --> 00:03:50.563 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
But a month later,

00:03:50.563 --> 00:03:52.966 align:start size:94% position:6%
when everything has dawned
on everyone

00:03:52.966 --> 00:03:55.268 size:75% position:38%
and we're now all --
I'm talking to you,

00:03:55.268 --> 00:03:58.771 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know, through
a computer screen, right?

00:03:58.771 --> 00:04:00.974 align:start size:94% position:6%
That for the Republicans
to think

00:04:00.974 --> 00:04:03.309 align:start size:94% position:6%
a low-turnout,
more difficult election

00:04:03.309 --> 00:04:04.043 size:81% position:41% line:92%
will aid their chances

00:04:04.043 --> 00:04:06.579 align:start size:94% position:6%
of winning
a state supreme court race -

00:04:06.579 --> 00:04:09.082 align:start size:94% position:6%
and if you want to vote,
then get out there, Milwaukee,

00:04:09.082 --> 00:04:10.283 line:92%
and stand in line with a mask,

00:04:10.283 --> 00:04:14.354 align:start size:94% position:6%
is so shockingly cynical
and monstrous

00:04:14.354 --> 00:04:17.123 align:start size:94% position:6%
that I could not believe
they did it, but they did it

00:04:17.123 --> 00:04:18.091 line:92%
and then everyone just assumed

00:04:18.091 --> 00:04:20.693
that those turnout effects
would help the Republican,

00:04:20.693 --> 00:04:23.329 align:start size:94% position:6%
and the Republican kind of
got his ass kicked.

00:04:23.329 --> 00:04:24.664 line:92%
Like, he lost by 10 points

00:04:24.664 --> 00:04:27.900 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, Donald Trump
won Wisconsin by 23,000 votes.

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:30.069 align:start size:94% position:6%
The liberal challenger
in this race

00:04:30.069 --> 00:04:32.305 align:start size:94% position:6%
who was running against
an incumbent

00:04:32.305 --> 00:04:33.406 line:92%
won by over 100,000 votes.

00:04:33.406 --> 00:04:37.510 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, this was a big, big wi
for the Democrats

00:04:37.510 --> 00:04:39.579 align:start size:94% position:6%
and liberals broadly
in Wisconsin.

00:04:39.579 --> 00:04:44.217
And I still don't really hav
my arms around why it happened

00:04:44.217 --> 00:04:45.218 size:88% position:44% line:92%
We talked to Ben Wikler,

00:04:45.218 --> 00:04:46.986 align:start size:94% position:6%
the chair of the
Democratic Party, last night

00:04:46.986 --> 00:04:47.887 size:81% position:41% line:92%
I think part of it was

00:04:47.887 --> 00:04:51.024 align:start size:94% position:6%
people were really furious
and pissed off that,

00:04:51.024 --> 00:04:51.724 line:92%
in the midst of pandemic,

00:04:51.724 --> 00:04:56.029
some people were, like, trying
to rig the game against them

00:04:56.029 --> 00:04:57.096 size:75% position:38% line:92%
-Well, it does seems

00:04:57.096 --> 00:04:58.998 align:start size:94% position:6%
like a bad bellwether
for the Republican Party.

00:04:58.998 --> 00:05:01.567 align:start size:94% position:6%
But I think,
as you referenced earlier,

00:05:01.567 --> 00:05:02.568 size:88% position:44% line:92%
it's impossible to know,

00:05:02.568 --> 00:05:04.003 align:start size:94% position:6%
based on anything
that happens this week,

00:05:04.003 --> 00:05:06.706
what that means in June,
what that means in October

00:05:06.706 --> 00:05:10.943 align:start size:94% position:6%
Do you feel like the Democrats
re doing enough right now

00:05:10.943 --> 00:05:13.746 align:start size:94% position:6%
to protect the election
in November?

00:05:13.746 --> 00:05:16.249 align:start size:94% position:6%
-That's a huge worry,
and the answer is probably no.

00:05:16.249 --> 00:05:17.817 size:84% position:42%
Although it is not that
they haven't tried --

00:05:17.817 --> 00:05:20.019 align:start size:94% position:6%
it's --
they haven't prioritized it.

00:05:20.019 --> 00:05:21.587 align:start size:94% position:6%
So there's a fight
in the CARES Act,

00:05:21.587 --> 00:05:25.591 align:start size:94% position:6%
which was the last rescue bill
that was passed,

00:05:25.591 --> 00:05:30.730 align:start size:94% position:6%
over funding
for universal mail-in voting

00:05:30.730 --> 00:05:31.864 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
And Democrats --

00:05:31.864 --> 00:05:33.599 align:start size:94% position:6%
Mitch McConnell's
giving $100 million,

00:05:33.599 --> 00:05:34.801 size:75% position:38%
and Democrats wanted
$2 billion dollars,

00:05:34.801 --> 00:05:37.103 align:start size:94% position:6%
and they ended up
getting north of $400 million.

00:05:37.103 --> 00:05:40.807 align:start size:94% position:6%
The problem is, like,
it's up to states in some ways

00:05:40.807 --> 00:05:42.342 align:start size:94% position:6%
They need the money
from the federal government,

00:05:42.342 --> 00:05:44.844 size:84% position:42%
but some states aren't
probably going to do it

00:05:44.844 --> 00:05:46.779 align:start size:94% position:6%
because
they don't want to do it.

00:05:46.779 --> 00:05:47.980 align:start size:94% position:6%
So you would probably
have to pass

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:50.950
some sort of universal mail-in
voting required legislation --

00:05:50.950 --> 00:05:53.052 align:start size:94% position:6%
Ron Wyden and Amy Klobucha
have worked on that.

00:05:53.052 --> 00:05:54.253 align:start size:94% position:6%
It think it has to be
a priority,

00:05:54.253 --> 00:05:59.225
because we don't know what's
going to happen in November.

00:05:59.225 --> 00:06:00.660 size:78% position:39% line:92%
Like, there may be --

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:01.894 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
In 1918, famously,

00:06:01.894 --> 00:06:03.830 align:start size:94% position:6%
the flu reared its head
in the spring,

00:06:03.830 --> 00:06:05.365 align:start size:94% position:6%
it diminished
through the summer,

00:06:05.365 --> 00:06:08.634 align:start size:94% position:6%
and then it had
its worst outbreak that fall

00:06:08.634 --> 00:06:12.171
And can you imagine
a 50-state version of Wisconsin?

00:06:12.171 --> 00:06:15.908 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, that -- it would jus
be both a public health

00:06:15.908 --> 00:06:17.443 align:start size:94% position:6%
and democratic legitimacy
debacle.

00:06:17.443 --> 00:06:20.279
It's a nightmare scenario
that I think Democrats

00:06:20.279 --> 00:06:23.950 align:start size:94% position:6%
need to focus much more energy
on preventing.

00:06:23.950 --> 00:06:25.318 line:92%
-Well, I look forward to you

00:06:25.318 --> 00:06:26.586 align:start size:94% position:6%
keeping us up to date
on all of this.

00:06:26.586 --> 00:06:28.821
So happy that you're still
managing to do your show.

00:06:28.821 --> 00:06:31.224 align:start size:94% position:6%
Thank you for making time
for us today.

00:06:31.224 --> 00:06:31.958 size:84% position:42% line:92%
Chris Hayes, everybody.

00:06:31.958 --> 00:06:34.927 align:start size:94% position:6%
Check out his show every night
-Great to be on, man.

